<p style="text-align: right">
“Reflection is the beginning of reform.”<br>
— Mark Twain
</p>

# Introduction # {#intro}

Formatting an enumeration as its underlying type currently requires either a
formatter specialization or an explicit conversion at every call site.
[[P3070]] proposed `format_as`, an ADL-based customization point that makes
this more convenient and allows the conversion to be performed before type
erasure.

Since P3070 was written, C++26 has gained reflection ([[P2996]]) and annotations
([[P3394]]). These provide a more ergonomic way to opt into formatting
behavior without introducing a new ADL customization point.

This paper supersedes P3070 and proposes annotation-based enum formatting,
including formatting enumerators as strings.

# Motivation and scope # {#motivation}

Consider the following `enum` from P3070:

```cpp
namespace kevin_namespacy {
enum class film {
  house_of_cards, american_beauty, se7en = 7
};
}
```

Formatting it as its underlying type currently requires a `formatter`
specialization:

```cpp
template <>
struct std::formatter<kevin_namespacy::film> : std::formatter<int> {
  auto format(kevin_namespacy::film f, std::format_context& ctx) const {
    return formatter<int>::format(std::to_underlying(f), ctx);
  }
};
```

This is unnecessarily verbose for a common operation.

Another option is to perform the conversion at the call site:

```cpp
film f = kevin_namespacy::se7en;
auto s = std::format("{}", std::to_underlying(f));
```

This reduces formatting overhead but requires the conversion to be repeated at
every call site.

P3070 tried to address both problems with an ADL customization:

```cpp
namespace kevin_namespacy {
enum class film {...};

auto format_as(film f) {
  return std::to_underlying(f);
}
}
```

This is more concise and allows the formatting implementation to convert the
enum before type erasure. However, ADL has well-known drawbacks and is no
longer necessary now that C++ has annotations.

# Problems with `format_as` # {#format-as-problems}

## ADL claims a generic name ## {#adl-name}

`format_as` is found by argument-dependent lookup. As a result, the otherwise
generic name `format_as` effectively becomes a formatting customization name in
associated namespaces.

For example:

```cpp
namespace lib {

struct widget {};

// An existing function unrelated to std::format.
int format_as(widget);

}
```

P3070 can cause this function to acquire formatting semantics even though it
was not written as a formatting customization.

This is not an explicit opt-in.

[[P2279]] discusses this problem for ADL-based customization points and notes
that ADL effectively requires globally reserving the customization identifier.
Two libraries cannot independently use the same ADL customization name without
the possibility of interaction.

An annotation does not have this problem:

```cpp
enum class [[=std::format_as_underlying]] film {
  // ...
};
```

The formatting behavior is explicitly attached to the declaration it affects.

## ADL pollutes the overload set ## {#adl-overloads}

ADL adds functions from associated namespaces and classes to the overload set.
For a class template specialization, associated entities can include the types
of its template arguments, making the lookup nonlocal.

For example, a call involving a type such as:

```cpp
wrapper<some_namespace::widget>
```

can consider `format_as` overloads from both the namespace containing `wrapper`
and the namespace containing `widget`.

This can increase compile time and make diagnostics include unrelated overload
candidates.

[[P2547]] discusses a more severe version of the same problem for
`tag_invoke`, where large ADL overload sets increase compilation cost and
diagnostic size. `format_as` is less problematic because only overloads with one
name participate, but there is no benefit in introducing this cost when the
customization can be expressed without overload resolution.

Annotations require neither lookup nor overload resolution.

## `format_as` requires unnecessary boilerplate for enums

For the common case of formatting an enum as its underlying type, the conversion
is already defined by the language. With format_as, the user must still define a
function that merely performs this conversion:

```cpp
constexpr auto format_as(color c) {
  return std::to_underlying(c);
}
```

An annotation can express the intent directly, without requiring a user-defined
conversion function.

## General `format_as` needs further design

P3070 proposes `format_as` as a general customization mechanism for
user-defined types, not just enumerations. That generality is useful, but the
proposed design leaves broader questions about interactions with existing
formatting customizations, constraints on the result type, and exception
specifications such as `noexcept`.

These issues should be addressed before standardizing a general `format_as`
facility. The annotation-based approach proposed here can be extended to
user-defined types in the future once these design questions have been
resolved.

The motivating enum use case does not require arbitrary conversion. Conversion
from an enum to its underlying type is defined by the language, nonthrowing,
and has no side effects.

# Argument mapping is not formatter forwarding # {#argument-mapping}

P3070 states that the semantics of `format_as` are the same as those of a
forwarding `formatter` specialization. This is not quite true when the
conversion is performed before type erasure.

Consider:

```cpp
enum class width {
  small = 4,
  large = 20
};

auto format_as(width w) {
  return std::to_underlying(w);
}
```

With argument mapping this can be used as:

```cpp
std::format("{:{}}", "foo", width::large);
```

because `width::large` is converted to an integer before construction of
`basic_format_arg`.

A forwarding formatter does not have this property:

```cpp
template <>
struct std::formatter<width> : std::formatter<int> {
  // ...
};
```

Dynamic width and precision require the corresponding format argument to have
an appropriate integer formatting argument type. A custom formatter does not
make the type an integer format argument.

Therefore there are two observably different facilities:

1. forwarding formatting of `E` to `formatter<underlying_type_t<E>>`; and
2. mapping `E` to `underlying_type_t<E>` as a formatting argument.

This paper deliberately proposes the second.

# Formatting as the underlying type # {#underlying}

This paper proposes the annotation:

```cpp
[[=std::format_as_underlying]]
```

which can be applied to an enumeration definition:

```cpp
enum class [[=std::format_as_underlying]] color {
  red   = 1,
  green = 2,
  blue  = 4
};
```

For formatting purposes, a value of such an enum is mapped as if
`std::to_underlying` had been applied before passing the argument to the
formatting facility.

For example:

```cpp
auto s1 = std::format("{}", color::green);     // "2"
auto s2 = std::format("{:04x}", color::blue);  // "0004"
```

The mapping also applies when an argument is used as dynamic width or precision:

```cpp
enum class [[=std::format_as_underlying]] width {
  small = 4,
  large = 10
};

auto s = std::format("{:{}}", 42, width::large);
// s == "        42"
```

This is an intentional part of the semantics and allows the conversion to be
performed before type erasure, improving efficiency.

The mapped value is type-erased as its mapped type, avoiding the custom-type
handle and formatter dispatch.

## `std::byte` ## {#byte}

`std::byte` is an important use case.

With the proposed behavior:

```cpp
std::format("{}", std::byte{42});       // "42"
std::format("{:x}", std::byte{42});     // "2a"
std::format("{:{}}", 1, std::byte{4});  // "   1"
```

`std::byte` retains its type safety outside formatting while behaving like its
underlying integer where the formatting facility specifically requires an
integer argument.

P3070 showed that mapping an enum to a built-in formatting argument before type
erasure can be approximately twice as fast as dispatching through a custom
formatter for this use case. The same speedup applies to this proposal because
it uses the same argument-mapping mechanism.

<pre>
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Benchmark                           Time             CPU   Iterations
---------------------------------------------------------------------
BM_Formatter                     17.7 ns         17.7 ns     38037070
BM_FormatAs                      8.90 ns         8.88 ns     79036210
</pre>

# Lightweight opt-in header # {#header}

Formatting annotations appear on type declarations, which are commonly placed
in widely included headers.

Requiring `<format>` merely to name an annotation would therefore impose the
compile-time cost of `<format>` on translation units that do not use formatting:

```cpp
// color.h
#include <format>

enum class [[=std::format_as_underlying]] color {
  red,
  green,
  blue
};
```

This paper instead proposes a lightweight `<format_annotations>` header
containing the annotation marker objects:

```cpp
#include <format_annotations>

enum class [[=std::format_as_underlying]] color {
  red,
  green,
  blue
};
```

`<format>` includes `<format_annotations>`.

The intended dependency is therefore:

```text
<format_annotations>
        ↑
     <format>
```

The lightweight header does not need to include `<format>` or `<meta>`. It only
declares the annotation values used at the type declaration.

This is particularly important for declaration-site customization because the
cost of the customization mechanism should not be paid by every translation
unit that sees the declaration.

# Formatting as an identifier # {#identifier}

Reflection enables another common enum formatting mode that does not require
user-written conversion code.

This paper additionally proposes:

```cpp
[[=std::format_as_identifier]]
```

For example:

```cpp
enum class [[=std::format_as_identifier]] color {
  red,
  green,
  blue
};

auto s = std::format("{}", color::green);  // "green"
```

The implementation obtains the enumerators with `std::meta::enumerators_of` and
their names with `std::meta::identifier_of`.

This is substantially more convenient than writing:

```cpp
auto format_as(color c) -> std::string_view {
  switch (c) {
  case color::red:   return "red";
  case color::green: return "green";
  case color::blue:  return "blue";
  }
}
```

and cannot become stale when enumerators are added.

## Aliases ## {#aliases}

Multiple enumerators can have the same value:

```cpp
enum class [[=std::format_as_identifier]] status {
  ok      = 0,
  success = 0
};
```

When multiple enumerators have the same value, the first matching enumerator is
used:

```cpp
std::format("{}", status::success);  // "ok"
```

There is no way to distinguish the two values at runtime.

## Values without a matching enumerator ## {#unmatched-values}

An enum value is not necessarily the value of an enumerator:

```cpp
auto c = static_cast<color>(42);
```

If no enumerator has the value being formatted, the value is represented as the
decimal value of the underlying type.

The resulting representation follows string formatting semantics:

```cpp
std::format("{:>5}", static_cast<color>(42));  // "   42"
```

Integer presentation specifiers such as `x` are not accepted for
`format_as_identifier`. Whether the value happens to match an enumerator is a
runtime property and cannot change the grammar accepted by the formatter.

Users who want integer formatting should use `format_as_underlying` instead.

## Interaction between the annotations ## {#annotation-interaction}

`format_as_underlying` and `format_as_identifier` specify different
representations and are mutually exclusive.

The following is ill-formed:

```cpp
enum class
  [[=std::format_as_underlying]]
  [[=std::format_as_identifier]]
color {
  red,
  green,
  blue
};
```

The two parts of this proposal are otherwise independent and can be polled
separately.

# Why annotations? # {#why-annotations}

Annotations have several useful properties for this facility.

First, the opt-in is local and explicit:

```cpp
enum class [[=std::format_as_underlying]] color {
  // ...
};
```

A reader can determine the enum's default formatting behavior from its
declaration.

Second, annotations do not introduce a customization name into associated
namespaces. There is no overload set, no ADL and no possibility that an
unrelated function accidentally becomes part of the formatting protocol.

Third, annotations are well suited to properties that apply to a declaration as
a whole. P3394 motivates annotations with declaration-level library
customization such as serialization, command-line parsing and test
parametrization. Formatting is another instance of the same pattern.

Finally, reflection makes identifier-based formatting possible without code
generation or manually maintained lookup tables.

# Prior art # {#prior-art}

Declaration-site opt-in for generated textual representations is common in
other languages.

Rust provides ([[RUST-DEBUG]]):

```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
enum Color {
    Red,
    Green,
    Blue,
}
```

Elixir provides `@derive Inspect` ([[ELIXIR-DERIVE]]), and Groovy provides
`@ToString` ([[GROOVY-TOSTRING]]). These mechanisms differ in implementation
but have the same general property: textual representation behavior is
requested declaratively on the type.

Other languages also distinguish symbolic and numeric enum representations. For
example, C# enum formatting provides both name-oriented and numeric forms
([[CSHARP-ENUM]]).

The proposed annotations bring a similar declarative model to C++ using the
C++26 reflection facilities.

# Implementation experience # {#implementation}

The underlying-type annotation can use the same format argument mapping that
{fmt} already uses for arithmetic `format_as` results, except that the
conversion is fixed to `std::to_underlying` and cannot invoke arbitrary user
code.

{fmt} also has a prototype for formatting enumerators as identifiers:

```cpp
enum class [[=fmt::as_identifiers]] color {
  red,
  green,
  blue
};
```

The implementation uses C++26 reflection to retrieve the enumerators and their
identifiers, with the annotation providing the opt-in.

# Proposed change # {#proposal}

Add a new header `<format_annotations>` with:

```cpp
namespace std {

struct format_as_underlying_t {};
inline constexpr format_as_underlying_t format_as_underlying;

struct format_as_identifier_t {};
inline constexpr format_as_identifier_t format_as_identifier;

}
```

`<format>` includes `<format_annotations>`.

An enumeration whose definition is annotated with `std::format_as_underlying`
is formatted by mapping the value to its underlying type before the normal
format argument mapping is applied.

An enumeration whose definition is annotated with `std::format_as_identifier`
is formatted using the identifier of the first enumerator with the same value.
If there is no such enumerator, its underlying value is converted to decimal.
Identifier formatting uses string format specifications.

An enumeration cannot have both annotations.

The first change applies to both `char` and `wchar_t` formatting because it
delegates to existing integral formatting.

Identifier formatting initially applies to `char` formatting.
`std::meta::identifier_of` produces a narrow string representation;
wide-character identifier formatting requires additional encoding specification
and can be added separately if there is demand.

# Wording # {#wording}

Add a new subclause [format.annotations] and header synopsis:

<pre>
// &lt;format_annotations>

namespace std {

  struct format_as_underlying_t {};
  inline constexpr format_as_underlying_t format_as_underlying;

  struct format_as_identifier_t {};
  inline constexpr format_as_identifier_t format_as_identifier;

}
</pre>

For an enumeration type `E`, let *format-as-underlying* be `true` if an
annotation whose underlying constant has type `format_as_underlying_t`
appertains to the definition of `E`, and `false` otherwise.

Let *format-as-identifier* be defined analogously for `format_as_identifier_t`.

An enumeration type shall not be both *format-as-underlying* and
*format-as-identifier*.

Modify the format argument mapping in
&bs[;[format.arg](https://eel.is/c++draft/format.arg)] so that if
`remove_const_t<T>` is an enumeration type for which *format-as-underlying* is
`true`, `v` is first mapped to `to_underlying(v)` and normal format argument
mapping is then applied to the result.

[Note: As a consequence, an enumeration annotated with `format_as_underlying`
can be used where the formatting facility requires an integer argument,
including dynamic width and precision. — end note]

Add an enabled specialization for identifier formatting:

<pre>
template&lt;class E>
  requires (is_enum_v&lt;E> &amp;&amp; <i>format-as-identifier</i>&lt;E>)
struct formatter&lt;E, char> {
private:
  formatter&lt;string_view, char> <i>fmt_</i>;  // <i>exposition only</i>

public:
  constexpr format_parse_context::iterator
    parse(format_parse_context&amp; ctx);

  template&lt;class FormatContext>
    typename FormatContext::iterator
      format(E value, FormatContext&amp; ctx) const;
};
</pre>

<pre>
constexpr format_parse_context::iterator
  parse(format_parse_context&amp; ctx);
</pre>

*Returns*: <code><i>fmt_</i>.parse(ctx)</code>.

<pre>
template&lt;class FormatContext>
  typename FormatContext::iterator
    format(E value, FormatContext&amp; ctx) const;
</pre>

Let `e` be the first element of `meta::enumerators_of(^^E)` whose
value compares equal to `value`.

If such an element exists, let `s` be `meta::identifier_of(e)`. Otherwise let
`s` be the decimal character representation of `to_underlying(value)` with no
additional formatting options.

*Returns*: <code><i>fmt_</i>.format(s, ctx)</code>.

Specify `std::byte` as *format-as-underlying*.

# Feature-test macro # {#feature-test}

Set the value of `__cpp_lib_format` to a value corresponding to the date
of adoption of this proposal.

